# Tillforty app boilerplate — Caddy reverse proxy (public HTTPS).
#
# Used only by the optional `caddy` service (compose profile `public`, enabled
# when DOMAIN is set in .env). Caddy forwards all traffic to the `web` (nginx)
# container, which serves the SPA and proxies /api to the backend.
#
# DOMAIN     — the public hostname, e.g. app.tillforty.com (required when used)
# ACME_EMAIL — contact email for Let's Encrypt (recommended; expiry notices)
# CADDY_TLS  — TLS strategy for the origin (see .env.example):
#                ""             automatic Let's Encrypt (server is internet-facing)
#                "tls internal" self-signed; for use behind a TLS-terminating
#                               proxy such as Cloudflare in "Full" SSL mode
#                "tls /cert /key" a custom/origin certificate

{
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

{$DOMAIN} {
	{$CADDY_TLS}
	encode gzip
	reverse_proxy web:80
}

# GlitchTip over TLS on a second port of the SAME hostname. A dedicated
# subdomain would need its own DNS record, which not every install can add; the
# certificate Caddy already holds for {$DOMAIN} covers this as-is, because a
# certificate is bound to the name and not to the port.
#
# Why this matters beyond tidiness: the browser SDK posts to VITE_SENTRY_DSN, and
# an http:// DSN from an https:// page is mixed active content that browsers
# block outright — so frontend error capture silently never arrives. Serving
# GlitchTip over TLS is what fixes that.
#
# Keep GLITCHTIP_PUBLIC_DOMAIN (.env) in step with this address, since it is the
# DSN host, the email-link host, and the CSRF origin all at once.
#
# {$DOMAIN} is always set when this file is used — the `public` profile that
# runs Caddy is only enabled when DOMAIN is present — and even unset it would
# degrade to `:8443` (a valid address, just without TLS) rather than the keyless
# block that would stop Caddy booting.
{$DOMAIN}:8443 {
	{$CADDY_TLS}
	encode gzip
	reverse_proxy glitchtip:8080
}

# n8n over TLS on the SAME hostname, reusing the certificate Caddy already holds
# for {$DOMAIN} — same reasoning as the GlitchTip block above (a certificate is
# bound to the name, not the port), so no extra DNS record is needed.
#
# Why this exists: n8n itself speaks plain http on 5678. Any browser with
# HTTPS-only mode, or an HSTS entry picked up from {$DOMAIN}, silently upgrades
# http://{$DOMAIN}:5678 to https:// — which a plain-http listener cannot answer,
# so the UI reads as "not reachable" while the container is perfectly healthy.
#
# Caddy owns the public :5678 here, so n8n's own publish is moved to a
# loopback-only port (N8N_BIND in .env) to avoid a bind conflict. Keep
# N8N_PUBLIC_URL, N8N_WEBHOOK_URL and N8N_PROTOCOL in step with this address.
{$DOMAIN}:5678 {
	{$CADDY_TLS}
	encode gzip
	# n8n's editor UI uses websockets for push; reverse_proxy upgrades them natively.
	reverse_proxy n8n:5678
}
